event('212-2.1','targeted proof check','Left exactness follows because F is Hom_S(Spec(Ω),-). For right exactness, work at s and make the flat strict henselization A to A^sh chosen in Ω. Flatness commutes with the equalizer D=ker(u-v) of finite A-algebras C to B. Use the standard henselian decomposition theorem: a finite algebra over a strictly henselian local ring is a finite product of local algebras, each with a purely inseparable residue extension, hence one Ω-point. Idempotents of D are exactly idempotents e of C with u(e)=v(e). These are the characteristic functions of subsets of the finite set of points of C saturated under the relation generated by the two maps from the points of B. Primitive idempotents of D therefore correspond precisely to the equivalence classes, which proves preservation of coequalizers. Finite sums are immediate. This proof uses flatness and the finite-algebra henselian decomposition theorem as named background inputs; it does not assume arbitrary base change commutes with equalizers.','212, pp. 103–104, Proposition 2.1')
event('212-2-finite-colimits-all-preschemes','targeted proof check','Continuation of the p. 102 background item using Proposition 2.1: for the equalizer algebra D inside C, C is finite over D, so q:Spec(C) to Spec(D) is finite surjective and is a topological quotient. Proposition 2.1 says its geometric fibres are exactly the equivalence classes generated by the original pair. A compatible map from Spec(C) to any prescheme Z is thus constant on these fibres and gives a continuous map g from Spec(D) to Z. On the inverse image of an affine open of Z, localization of D=Eq(C to B) supplies the unique ring maps on principal opens, and they glue. Their stalk maps are local: a function vanishing at g(y) vanishes at the points above y, so its descended value lies in the maximal ideal at y. This gives a unique scheme morphism and proves the coequalizer property for arbitrary targets. The construction glues over S; finite coproducts are already absolute. The previous affine-only gap is therefore supplied, conditional on the named henselian input in the proof of 2.1.','212, p. 104, Proposition 2.1, applied to the final claim on p. 102')
event('212-2-kernel-comparison','targeted proof check','For a finite equivalence relation R on X, Proposition 2.1 identifies the geometric points of Y=X/R with the set-theoretic classes. Left exactness identifies the geometric points of X times_Y X with pairs in a class. Since R is an equivalence relation, the comparison R to X times_Y X is therefore bijective on all geometric points; it is finite and a monomorphism. Using the standard fact that a finite monomorphism is a closed immersion, the comparison is a surjective closed immersion and in particular radicial. This proves the concrete consequence independently of the informal description of the localized category as geometric.','212, p. 104, paragraph after Proposition 2.1')
event('212-2-geometric-category','carried as background without fault found','The claim that localization at surjective radicial morphisms has the same properties of finite nature as sets is informal as printed. A precise version must specify the finite exactness axioms and prove their transport through the localization, including the required calculus of fractions. The concrete kernel-pair consequence was checked separately; no complete categorical localization theorem is claimed here.','212, p. 104, localized-category paragraph')
event('212-3-group-action-definition','targeted proof check','Yoneda identifies natural multiplication on G(T) and natural action on X(T) with the displayed morphisms whenever G times G and G times X exist. Conversely applying Hom(T,-) to them gives the laws. The condition that the induced laws be a group and group action for every T forces units and inverses to be natural: any multiplication-preserving map between groups preserves the unique idempotent identity and then inverses. Equality of the relevant natural transformations is equivalent by Yoneda to the associativity/action diagrams when their products exist. No terminal object is silently assumed in the original functorial definition.','212, p. 104, section 3')
record_page('212',104,r'''Compared sources/fr_printed/fr/212.tex and sources/fr/fr/212.tex entry lines 224–271 with scan leaf 7 at 400 dpi, full page and all three enlarged bands. Checked the slash and capital S in X/S, every prime and subscript, the plain C and C' in the localization paragraph versus wavy C below, the inline kernel-pair comparison, both displayed laws and the final double arrow.

Both layers restore X/S in place of the supplied X_s, the comma after finite sur S, ordinary quotes and arrows, i. e. spacing, selective underlines, the roman underlined heading and its period, the inline comparison, absence of terminal comma in the laws display, the comma after fait, and upright variables. The local plain category letters remain plain. No original misprint is emended on this page.

Checked Proposition 2.1 by strict henselization and idempotents, naming the henselian decomposition input explicitly. Used it to supply the arbitrary-target coequalizer argument left open on p. 102 and to prove the surjective closed-immersion comparison. Kept the broader informal localization claim as background. Checked the group-action formulation by Yoneda.''')
